HOW_TO_READ = ('<b>How to read a box.</b> Top: the class name (dashed border = interface; &laquo;enum&raquo; = a fixed '
 'list of values). Middle: its fields, the state it holds. Bottom: its methods. <b>The arrows.</b> Hollow triangle = '
 'implements. Filled diamond = owns: the service owns every order and every restaurant, an order owns its lines, a '
 'restaurant owns its menu. Plain arrow = references: an order points at its rider <i>by id</i>, because the rider '
 'outlives the order and belongs to the fleet. Dashed lavender = handed in through <code>configure()</code> &mdash; the '
 'four drawn here are the pricing chain, the dispatcher, the gateway and the clock, and the cancellation policy and '
 'the demand signal arrive the same way. Dotted blue = notifies. <b>Where the state lives, and where the locks are:</b> '
 'the restaurant has the menu, the stock counts and one <code>ReentrantLock</code>; each order has its own lock for its '
 'own state; a rider has a single <code>AtomicReference</code> flag and no lock at all; the service itself holds no '
 'lock, only maps and the rules it was handed. Notice what is <i>not</i> here: no Payment class (the gateway is an '
 'interface, and an order keeps only the charge id), no Notification class, and no separate Menu class &mdash; a menu '
 'is two maps inside the restaurant that owns them.')

# ============================================================ page 04: the code
CODE_INTRO = ('Read it with page 03 open in a second tab if you want the diagram beside it. The green comment above each '
 'class and method says what it does; read only those first for the shape, then the bodies for the mechanics. Each copy '
 'button copies that whole file for your IDE. Below Main.java: Extensions.java (every follow-up\'s reference code, with '
 'an <code>ExtDemo</code> main that runs all of it) and FailureTests.java (twenty-seven checks proven; '
 '<code>javac Main.java Extensions.java FailureTests.java &amp;&amp; java FailureTests</code> prints ALL PASS).')

# ============================================================ page 05: follow-ups and practice
IMPLEMENT_CARD = ('<div class="card"><div class="ch"><h3>0 &middot; Implement the system</h3>'
 '<button class="timer" data-min="60">start 60:00</button></div><div class="cb"><div class="prompt">' + PROMPT + '</div>'
 'Before typing, write your six to eight clarifying questions (one restaurant per cart, and whether payment is in '
 'scope, first); then type in the order of Main.java: OrderState with its transition table, PartnerState, Money, the '
 'records (Geo, MenuItem, OrderLine, Bill, PriceCtx, Refund), the six one-method interfaces, the pricing rules with '
 'the two decorators, NearestFree and StandardCancellation, Restaurant with its lock and <code>reserve()</code>, Cart, '
 'Order with its own lock, DeliveryPartner with its compare-and-set, then OrderService with placeOrder in its five '
 'numbered steps, then a main with thirty threads racing for ten portions.</div></div>')

FU = [
("Marketing wants 40% off, capped at 120 rupees, on a bill that already has a distance fee and a surge multiplier on it.",
 "functional", 10,
 "The price is a list of one-method rules applied in order, so this is two new classes and two lines. "
 "<code>PercentCoupon</code> takes its percentage off the food only; <code>CappedDiscount</code> wraps it and trims "
 "whatever it took off down to the cap, so &ldquo;40% off, up to 120&rdquo; is a wrapper rather than a clause inside "
 "every coupon written from now on. The order of the list is the order of the maths: food, then the distance fee, then "
 "surge on the fee only, then the coupons. On the worked example that is 558.00 of food, 44.00 of fee (20 plus 8 a "
 "kilometre, rounded up to 3), 22.00 of surge, and 223.20 of coupon trimmed to 120.00, for 504.00. Every rule is "
 "wrapped in <code>CheckedRule</code> before it is used, so a rule that leaves the parts and the total out of step "
 "throws before the card is touched.",
 sect(src, "final class DistanceFee", "final class CheckedRule")),
("Two customers tap for the last two portions at the same instant. Prove one of them loses, with a test.",
 "non-functional", 10,
 "The race lives between reading a stock count and writing it back. <code>Restaurant.reserve</code> does the whole "
 "read-modify-write inside the restaurant's lock, and in two phases: check <i>every</i> line against the live menu and "
 "the live stock first, and only then decrement them all, so an order is all of its lines or none of them. The proof is "
 "a count, not a hope: thirty threads wait on one latch, each wants two of the ten portions, and afterwards exactly "
 "five orders exist, the stock is exactly zero and the card was touched exactly five times. The last number is the one "
 "worth pointing at &mdash; it proves the twenty-five losers never reached the gateway at all.",
 T("        // 1. thirty phones", "        // 2. one unavailable line")),
("One lock per restaurant. Does that scale, or have you serialised Swiggy?",
 "non-functional", 5,
 "It scales, and the answer is arithmetic. The locked part is about ten hash operations &mdash; the open flag, k lines "
 "against two maps, k decrements, one insert &mdash; roughly two microseconds. A busy kitchen does four orders a minute "
 "at lunch; even a fictional one taking fifty checkouts a second holds its lock a hundred microseconds in every second, "
 "one hundredth of one per cent. The lock is per restaurant, so the eight thousand restaurants in the city never meet. "
 "The thing that would ruin this is charging the card inside the lock: four hundred milliseconds is two hundred "
 "thousand times longer than the lock, and at four orders a minute you would never see it in testing. The ladder, in "
 "order: keep payment and notification outside the lock; then an atomic counter per item so two dishes never wait; then "
 "a row per item with <code>UPDATE menu SET left = left - ? WHERE id = ? AND left &gt;= ?</code>.",
 sect(src, "List<OrderLine> reserve(Map<String, Integer> want)", "void giveBack(List<OrderLine> lines)")),
("The card is declined at checkout. What is the state of the system?",
 "functional", 10,
 "Exactly what it was. The order is built <i>after</i> the charge returns, so a decline leaves no order in the map, "
 "nothing on the restaurant's screen and no refund to issue. What it does leave is a reservation, so the catch hands "
 "the stock straight back under the restaurant's lock and rethrows; the customer sees one clean error and can try "
 "another card. The same exit covers a sold-out line and a pricing rule that throws. The two places worth defending out "
 "loud: the card is charged with no lock held, because four hundred milliseconds inside a lock would stall every other "
 "customer of that restaurant, and a gateway that throws rather than returning false is treated as a failure, which is "
 "the safe reading &mdash; a charge whose outcome is unknown is reconciled later, not assumed.",
 sect(src, "private Order doPlace", "void accept(String orderId)")),
("The app asks 'where is my order?' a thousand times a second. Make it O(1), with no lock.",
 "non-functional", 5,
 "No new structure. The order is one hash lookup by id, and its state and its rider are <code>volatile</code> fields, "
 "so the read path takes no lock at all &mdash; a reader sees either the old state or the new one, never a torn value, "
 "which is all a status screen needs. The restaurant's live queue is an insertion-ordered set, so adding and removing "
 "are O(1) and it still reads in arrival order for the tablet. The only thing that takes the order's lock on a read is "
 "<code>stamps()</code>, which copies the timestamp map, and that is deliberate: it is read once when somebody opens "
 "the receipt, not a thousand times a second.",
 sect(src, "    private final Map<String, Restaurant> restaurants", "    private List<PricingRule> pricing")
 + "\n" + sect(src, "Order order(String id)", "Cart openCart(")),
("The customer cancels. Sometimes it is free, sometimes there is a fee, and sometimes it is too late.",
 "twist", 10,
 "Two rules, in two places, each where it belongs. <i>Whether</i> a cancel is allowed is the transition table: "
 "CANCELLED is a successor of PLACED, ACCEPTED, PREPARING and READY, and is not a successor of PICKED_UP, so once the "
 "rider has the food the attempt throws instead of silently succeeding. <i>How much comes back</i> is an injected "
 "policy, asked inside the order's lock and given the state it is cancelling out of, so a state change racing the "
 "refund cannot fool it: free before the kitchen starts, and the total minus a flat fee once it is cooking. The stock "
 "follows the same logic &mdash; an order cancelled before ACCEPTED puts its food back on the shelf, and one cancelled "
 "while cooking does not, because that food exists now. Then the refund, the queue removal and the rider hand-back all "
 "happen outside the lock.",
 sect(src, "Refund cancel(String orderId)", "private OrderState transition(")
 + "\n" + sect(src, "final class StandardCancellation", "final class FakeGateway")),
("Two orders become ready in the same millisecond and one rider is nearest to both. And what if nobody is free at all?",
 "non-functional", 10,
 "Ranking and claiming are deliberately separate. The assignment rule ranks a <i>snapshot</i> of the free riders and "
 "returns a list, which may already be stale by the time it returns &mdash; and that is fine, because the winner is "
 "decided by <code>claim()</code>, a single compare-and-set on the rider's own flag, not by the ranking. The loser of "
 "the CAS simply walks to the next candidate, and re-snapshots if it runs out. Nobody free is a normal outcome rather "
 "than an exception: the order joins a waiting deque and <code>markReady</code> returns false. Every terminal path "
 "&mdash; delivered, cancelled, rejected &mdash; hands the rider back and gives the next waiting order a chance, which "
 "is the line most people forget: miss it and the fleet works perfectly for one delivery each and is dead by the second.",
 sect(src, "boolean claim(String forOrderId)", "void release()")
 + "\n" + sect(src, "private boolean tryAssign", "void deliver(String orderId)")
 + "\n" + sect(src, "private void handBack(Order o)", "private void publish(")),
("When will it arrive? And where does the time come from, and where does live GPS go?",
 "design", 8,
 "The ETA is a pure function of state: what the kitchen still owes (prep time minus how long it has been cooking) plus "
 "the ride at a speed handed in. It reads the order's own timestamps and an injected clock, so a test can set the "
 "instant, place an order, move the clock ten minutes and assert the exact number of minutes left. Nothing else in the "
 "system reads the wall clock. Live GPS is deliberately <i>not</i> an observer: state changes happen six times an hour "
 "and are event-shaped, while positions arrive a few times a second, so the location stream gets its own topic per "
 "order and touches no lock. Push them through the same callbacks and the status listeners drown.",
 X("eta", "multi-restaurant basket") + "\n"
 + "// where time comes from: one interface, handed in, defaulted, never read from the wall clock inside a method\n"
 + "interface Clock { long nowMs(); }\n"
 + "private Clock clock = System::currentTimeMillis;\n"
 + "void setClock(Clock c) { clock = c; }\n\n"
 + "// in a test: pick the instant, then move it\n"
 + "long[] now = { 1_700_000_000_000L };\n"
 + "svc.setClock(() -> now[0]);\n"
 + "now[0] += 10 * 60_000;                                 // ten minutes later\n\n"
 + X("live tracking", "persistence")),
("One tap, two restaurants: biryani from one and dessert from another.",
 "twist", 5,
 "It fans out into one order per restaurant, because stock, prep time and the rider all belong to a restaurant; a "
 "single order spanning two kitchens would have two prep times and two pickups and no honest state machine. So the "
 "basket places each order in turn with a key derived from the one tap. The interesting part is the failure: there is "
 "no distributed transaction here, so if the second restaurant is out of stock the first order is cancelled, which "
 "refunds it through the ordinary path. Say that out loud rather than hiding it &mdash; compensation is the answer, and "
 "the alternative (reserve everywhere, then pay once) is a two-phase commit you do not want in an hour.",
 X("multi-restaurant basket", "scheduled orders")),
("A rider accepts and then does not show. And can one rider carry two drops on the same trip?",
 "twist", 10,
 "Push assignment lies a little, so an offer holds the rider, asks him with a deadline, and hands him straight back on "
 "a no or on silence &mdash; the release happens before the method returns, so the pool never leaks. The refusal is "
 "recorded, and the ranking rule pushes a flaky rider down the list by charging him half a kilometre per miss, which is "
 "a new <code>AssignmentRule</code> and nothing else. Batching is the smaller change of the two: the rider's state was "
 "a flag with two values, so a compare-and-set fitted; with capacity it becomes a counter, so the same single "
 "indivisible claim becomes <code>Semaphore.tryAcquire</code>. The queue, the ranking and the terminal release do not "
 "move at all.",
 X("the rider says no", "a new pricing rule") + "\n" + X("pooling", "the rider says no")),
("Scheduled orders: 'deliver at eight', booked at four in the afternoon.",
 "twist", 5,
 "Not a new state. A booking sits outside the order model until the kitchen has to start, which is the requested time "
 "minus that restaurant's prep time; at that point the scheduler places an ordinary order and everything downstream is "
 "unchanged. The part worth pointing at is the id: it is derived from the booking, so a tick that runs twice, or two "
 "servers running the same job, place it exactly once &mdash; the same idempotency that makes a retried tap safe. What "
 "you should <i>not</i> do is reserve the stock at booking time; the kitchen will restock before then, and holding a "
 "portion for four hours is how a restaurant goes sold out at six.",
 X("scheduled orders", "pooling")),
("Persist it. And now there are two servers.",
 "twist", 10,
 "The order's lock only guards one process, so it becomes a conditional UPDATE and the database performs the same "
 "atomic step: <code>UPDATE orders SET state = ? WHERE id = ? AND state = ?</code>, where zero rows updated means "
 "another server moved it first &mdash; exactly the error the in-memory guard throws. The insert carries the "
 "idempotency key with ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING, so a retried tap still creates one order. Stock moves the same way: "
 "<code>SET left = left - ? WHERE left &gt;= ?</code>. The one genuinely new piece is the outbox: the notification is "
 "written as a row in the <i>same transaction</i> as the state change and drained by a separate sender, because a "
 "process that commits a state and then dies before the push is the one failure the in-memory try/catch cannot cover.",
 X("persistence", "Runs every extension")),
("Which pattern is where, which SOLID letter is where, and where would a Factory or a Builder earn its place?",
 "design", 8,
 "None of the patterns was chosen up front; each is what a move produced. Strategy is move 3: pricing, dispatch and the "
 "refund policy are rules that will change. Chain is the same move's shape, a list of rules applied in order. Decorator "
 "is the same move's wrapper, twice: <code>CappedDiscount</code> caps any coupon, and <code>CheckedRule</code> polices "
 "every rule including the ones written next year. Observer is move 4's rule that a push must never be inside a lock. "
 "State is move 6, the transition table. For SOLID: S is move 2 (the restaurant owns stock, the order owns its state, "
 "the service owns the order of operations); O is a membership rule, a new file and one line; L is that the service "
 "calls <code>rule.apply</code> and never asks which rule it got; I is six interfaces with one method each, so a fake "
 "is a lambda; D is <code>configure()</code> and <code>setClock()</code>, which is why a test can hand in a gateway "
 "that declines everything. Factory earns its place the day coupons arrive as rows in a config table, because the "
 "pricing list is already the registry. Builder earns its place the day an order gains optional fields &mdash; a tip, "
 "a cooking note, packaging, gift wrap; today all seven are required, so a builder would be ceremony.",
 "// Strategy: a rule behind an interface, handed in, never built by the service\n"
 "interface PricingRule { Bill apply(Bill running, PriceCtx ctx); }\n"
 "void configure(List<PricingRule> pricing, AssignmentRule assignment,\n"
 "               CancellationPolicy cancellation, PaymentProcessor payments) { /* wraps each rule */ }\n\n"
 "// Chain: the bill grows one rule at a time, in an order you can read off the list\n"
 "Bill bill = Bill.EMPTY;\n"
 "for (PricingRule rule : pricing) bill = rule.apply(bill, ctx);\n\n"
 "// Decorator, twice: one caps any coupon, one polices every rule including the ones written next year\n"
 "new CappedDiscount(new PercentCoupon(\"TASTY40\", 4000), Money.rupees(\"120.00\"));\n"
 "for (PricingRule r : pricing) checked.add(new CheckedRule(r));\n\n"
 "// Observer: the service announces; it does not know what a phone is\n"
 "private void publish(Order o, OrderState from) {\n"
 "    for (OrderObserver ob : observers) {\n"
 "        try { ob.onState(o, from); } catch (RuntimeException e) { /* log */ }\n"
 "    }\n"
 "}\n\n"
 "// State: the legal moves are data, so an illegal one cannot be written\n"
 "NEXT.put(PICKED_UP, EnumSet.of(DELIVERED));            // on the road: no going back\n"
 "if (!from.canGoTo(to)) throw new IllegalStateException(...);\n\n"
 "// Factory: not yet. The list IS the registry; it earns the name when coupons come from config rows\n"
 "List<PricingRule> fromConfig = rows.stream().map(PricingRules::of).toList();\n\n"
 "// Builder: not yet. Seven required fields is a constructor; a tip, a note and packaging would change that\n"
 "new Order(id, customerId, restaurantId, chargeId, lines, bill, dropAt, placedAtMs);\n"),
]

